<p>This chapter applies once an extension that brings a catalogue is running (for example for services, auctions, or a freelance market). Without such an extension there are no offers, no providers and no orders, and the "Marketplace" section is missing from the menu.</p>

<h2>Becoming a provider</h2>
<p>An account becomes a provider through a profile of its own, with a name, an address and legal details. Under <strong>Administration → Providers</strong>, profiles are approved, rejected or suspended. If an already-approved provider changes their legal details, that is flagged for a new review while the profile stays public.</p>

<h2>Making an offer</h2>
<p>A new offer is made in six steps, one screen at a time, not on one long form: first the languages (the account's own is always included), then the category, title and short text per chosen language, the description, the type's own fields, and finally a review before saving. The offer is then a draft; pictures are added on its edit page, and <strong>Submit</strong> sends it for review.</p>
<p>With approval switched off under <strong>Settings</strong>, a new offer is published at once. With it on, <strong>Administration → Offers</strong> decides to approve or reject.</p>

<h2>Categories</h2>
<p>Under <strong>Administration → Categories</strong> is the category tree offers are filed under, in every language.</p>

<h2>Orders and withdrawal</h2>
<p>Under <strong>Administration → Orders</strong> is every order with its history, messages, attachments and payments. Where the order flow allows it, an order can be moved on by hand. <strong>Withdrawals</strong> shows buyers' withdrawal declarations; each is passed on to the provider and ticked off as handled.</p>

<h2>Reviews and reports</h2>
<p>Under <strong>Moderation → Reviews</strong>, a review can be hidden with a reason, and its author is told. <strong>Reports</strong> are notices about content, also decided with a reason; reporters and the affected provider are told the outcome. The <strong>word filter</strong> sets which words may not appear in messages, questions to providers, order messages and reviews; a list of one's own replaces the shipped one, and <strong>Restore the default list</strong> brings it back.</p>
